    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Hereditary succession had no precedent in Islamic history, and Yazid's nomination by Mu'awiya was refused by Husayn and by the sons of other prominent companions of Muhammad.</li><li>Husayn's companions numbered thirty-two horsemen and forty infantrymen in most accounts, against an army of 4,000 that Shia sources say grew to 30,000.</li><li>The battle is central to Shia history and tradition, remembered every year in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muharram">Muharram</a> and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashura">Ashura</a>, and modern historians still disagree about what Husayn intended.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Theophilus of Edessa's history, preserved in later Christian chronicles, corroborates the death of Husayn and most of his men at Karbala after suffering from thirst, but appears to say that he was killed by Mu'awiya as the final engagement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-fitna">First Fitna</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

