    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">661</span><span class="rf-text">Ali is assassinated, and his son Hasan signs a treaty handing power to Mu'awiya</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">676</span><span class="rf-text">Mu'awiya nominates his son Yazid as his successor</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 680</span><span class="rf-text">Mu'awiya dies, and Yazid demands allegiance from Husayn and other dissidents</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 680</span><span class="rf-text">Husayn leaves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> with about fifty men and his family</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 680</span><span class="rf-text">Husayn is stopped by Kufan troops and camps at Karbala, where a second army arrives</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 680</span><span class="rf-text">The Kufan army attacks on 10 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muharram">Muharram</a>, and Husayn is killed with most of his companions</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">685</span><span class="rf-text">The Tawwabin, who rose in remorse for Husayn's death, are mostly killed at Ayn al-Warda</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">686</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mukhtar-al-thaqafi">Mukhtar al-Thaqafi</a>'s forces rout an Umayyad army at Khazir, and Ibn Ziyad is slain</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-karbala" data-v="c94ea51c68" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>Both sides take up positions</b> After the morning prayer both sides formed up. Husayn put Zuhayr ibn al-Qayn on his right and Habib ibn Muzahir on his left, with the tents tied together and the ditch behind them. The Kufan wings stood opposite, with 500 horsemen on the route to the river.</li><li><b>Volleys of arrows and duels</b> Zuhayr ibn al-Qayn tried to dissuade the Kufan soldiers from killing Husayn, but failed. The Kufans fired several volleys of arrows, and in the duels that followed several of Husayn's companions were slain.</li><li><b>The Kufan right wing attacks and is repulsed</b> The right wing of the Kufans, under Amr ibn al-Hajjaj, attacked Husayn's force and was repulsed. Hand-to-hand fighting then paused and further volleys of arrows were exchanged.</li><li><b>Shimr attacks and is repulsed</b> Shimr, who commanded the left wing of the Umayyad army, launched an attack. There were losses on both sides, and he was repulsed.</li><li><b>Cavalry attacks and Ibn Sa'd brings in reinforcements</b> Cavalry attacks followed, and Husayn's cavalry resisted fiercely. Ibn Sa'd brought in armoured cavalry and five hundred archers.</li><li><b>Husayn's cavalrymen dismount</b> Arrows wounded the horses of Husayn's cavalrymen, who dismounted and fought on foot. The Kufan archers kept shooting at both of Husayn's flanks.</li><li><b>The tents are burned</b> Because the Kufans could approach only from the front, Ibn Sa'd ordered the tents burned, except the one Husayn and his family used. The flames hindered the Umayyad advance for a while.</li><li><b>Husayn's companions are encircled</b> After noon prayers Husayn's companions were encircled, and almost all of them were killed. The Kufan wings closed on the two flanks from north and south.</li><li><b>Husayn's relatives join the fighting</b> Husayn's relatives, who had not fought until then, joined the battle. His son Ali al-Akbar was killed, then his half-brothers, including Abbas, and sons of Aqil ibn Abi Talib, Ja'far ibn Abi Talib and Hasan ibn Ali.</li><li><b>Husayn goes towards the river</b> The Umayyad soldiers hesitated to attack Husayn directly, but he was struck in the mouth by an arrow as he went to the river to drink. Later he was surrounded and struck on the head by Malik ibn Nusayr.</li><li><b>Shimr advances on Husayn</b> Few people were left on Husayn's side. Shimr advanced towards him with a group of foot soldiers, and a young boy from Husayn's camp tried to defend him from a sword stroke.</li><li><b>Husayn is killed</b> Shimr urged the Kufan soldiers on, and they rushed at Husayn and wounded him on his hand and shoulder. He fell face-down and was then stabbed and decapitated by Sinan ibn Anas.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the assassination of the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uthman-ibn-affan">Uthman ibn Affan</a> in 656, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a>, a cousin and son-in-law of Muhammad, was proclaimed fourth caliph in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. Uthman's kinsman Mu'awiya ibn Abi Sufyan, the long-time governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, denounced Ali's legitimacy. When Ali was assassinated in 661, his eldest son Hasan succeeded him but soon signed a peace treaty that handed power to Mu'awiya, on the condition that Mu'awiya be a just ruler and allow his successor to be chosen through shura (consultation). After Hasan died in 670, his younger brother Husayn became head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hashim">Banu Hashim</a> clan. Ali's supporters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> gave him their allegiance, but he abided by the treaty while Mu'awiya lived.</p><p>In 676 Mu'awiya nominated his son Yazid as successor, a move that has been labelled a breach of the treaty. Hereditary succession had no precedent in Islamic history and aroused opposition. Husayn, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr">Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr</a>, Abd Allah ibn Umar and Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, all sons of prominent companions of Muhammad, refused to support it. According to the historians Fitzpatrick and Walker, Yazid's succession, which was considered an "anomaly in Islamic history", changed the government from a consultative form to a monarchy. Before his death in April 680, Mu'awiya warned Yazid that Husayn and Ibn al-Zubayr might challenge his rule, and advised him to treat Husayn with caution and not to spill his blood, since he was the grandson of Muhammad.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Karbala&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Prelude:</p><ul><li>Bernard Lewis, Arabs in History (Oxford University Press, 2002)</li><li>S. M. Jafri, Origins and Early Development of Shi'a Islam (Longman, 1979)</li><li>Wilferd Madelung, The Succession to Muhammad: A Study of the Early Caliphate (Cambridge University Press, 1997)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On his accession Yazid told the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, al-Walid ibn Utba, to secure allegiance from Husayn and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr">Ibn al-Zubayr</a>, by force if necessary. Husayn declined to pledge allegiance in a private meeting and left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. There he received letters from pro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alids">Alids</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, who strongly resented Umayyad rule, asking him to lead a revolt. He sent his cousin Muslim ibn Aqil to assess their support. Yazid installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubayd-allah-ibn-ziyad">Ubayd Allah ibn Ziyad</a> as governor of Kufa, Ibn Aqil's following dissipated, and his revolt was crushed and he was killed. Husayn left Mecca on 9 September 680 with fifty men and his family, against the advice of Abd Allah ibn Abbas and Ibn al-Zubayr.</p><p>Near al-Qadisiyyah his caravan was intercepted by about 1,000 men of the Kufan army under al-Hurr ibn Yazid al-Tamimi, and forced to halt in a desolate place. On 2 October 680 Husayn camped at Karbala. The next day another army of 4,000 arrived under Umar ibn Sa'd, governor of Ray. Husayn's companions, according to most accounts, numbered thirty-two horsemen and forty infantrymen; some sources report forty-five horsemen and a hundred foot-soldiers, or a few hundred men in all. Ibn Sa'd's army totalled 4,000, but Shia sources say that more troops had joined it in preceding days, swelling it to 30,000.</p><p>On 10 October Husayn appointed Zuhayr ibn al-Qayn to command his right flank, Habib ibn Muzahir the left and his half-brother Abbas as standard bearer. His camp's tents had been tied together and a ditch dug behind them and filled with wood, to be set alight if attacked.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ibn Ziyad ordered that Husayn and his companions be denied access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. Ibn Sa'd stationed 500 horsemen on the route, and Husayn's camp stayed without water for three days before fifty men led by Abbas reached the river and filled twenty water-skins. Ibn Ziyad, persuaded by Shimr ibn Dhi al-Jawshan, ordered that Husayn give allegiance or be attacked. On the evening of 9 October Ibn Sa'd agreed to wait until morning, and Husayn told his men that they were free to leave under cover of night. Very few did.</p><p>After the morning prayer on 10 October both sides took position, and the ditch was set alight. Husayn addressed his opponents, reproached them for abandoning him after inviting him, and asked to leave. He was told he must first submit to Yazid, which he refused. Al-Hurr defected to him. Ibn Sa'd's army fired volleys of arrows, followed by duels in which several of Husayn's companions were killed. Attacks by the Kufan right wing and by Shimr on the left were repulsed, and cavalry attacks followed. Ibn Sa'd ordered all the tents but Husayn's burned, but the flames hindered his own advance for a while.</p><p>After noon prayers Husayn's companions were encircled and almost all were killed. His relatives then joined the fighting, and his son Ali al-Akbar, his half-brothers, including Abbas, and other kinsmen were slain. A young child of Husayn's, sitting on his lap, was hit by an arrow and died. Husayn was struck in the mouth by an arrow. Shimr shouted at the hesitating soldiers to kill him; they wounded him, and he was stabbed and decapitated by Sinan ibn Anas.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Seventy or seventy-two people died on Husayn's side, about twenty of them descendants of Abu Talib, the father of Ali. Eighty-eight died in Ibn Sa'd's army, and were buried before he left. Husayn's clothes, sword and baggage were taken, and the women's jewellery and cloaks. Reports give more than sixty wounds on Husayn's body, which was trampled with horses as Ibn Ziyad had instructed, and the bodies of his companions were decapitated. Members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-asad">Banu Asad</a> tribe buried the headless bodies after Ibn Sa'd had gone. Shimr wanted to execute Husayn's only surviving son, Ali Zayn al-Abidin, who was ill and had not fought, but Ibn Sa'd prevented it.</p><p>Husayn's family, with the heads of the dead, was sent to Ibn Ziyad and then to Yazid. Both are reported to have poked Husayn's mouth with a stick, which Henri Lammens suggests is a duplication. Yazid is reported to have been compassionate towards the women and Ali Zayn al-Abidin and to have cursed Ibn Ziyad. After a few days the women were compensated for their looted belongings and sent back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, Alid partisans who felt guilty at having abandoned Husayn formed the Tawwabin under Sulayman ibn Surad, with the slogan "Revenge for Husayn". Of 16,000 who enlisted, only 4,000 assembled, and most, including Ibn Surad, were killed at Ayn al-Warda in January 685. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mukhtar-al-thaqafi">Mukhtar al-Thaqafi</a> then took Kufa and executed those involved in Husayn's death, among them Ibn Sa'd and Shimr. His forces routed an Umayyad army at Khazir in August 686, and Ibn Ziyad was slain. Mukhtar was killed in April 687.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The primary source of the Karbala narrative is the Kitab Maqtal al-Husayn of the Kufan historian Abu Mikhnaf, who was an adult by 701 and knew many eyewitnesses. Most of them came from Ibn Sa'd's army, since few people from Husayn's camp survived. According to Julius Wellhausen, many regretted their part and embellished the accounts in Husayn's favour to dilute their guilt. Abu Mikhnaf's original seems to be lost, and the extant version has come through secondary sources such as al-Tabari and al-Baladhuri. The sources are mainly historical in nature, in contrast to later literature, which is mainly hagiographical, though early sources contain some miraculous stories.</p><p>An early Christian source also reports the battle. The history of Theophilus of Edessa, partly preserved in the chronicles of Michael the Syrian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theophanes-the-confessor">Theophanes the Confessor</a>, corroborates the death of Husayn and most of his men after suffering from thirst, but appears to say that he was killed by Mu'awiya, not Yazid, as the final engagement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-fitna">First Fitna</a>.</p><p>Lammens, relying on an official report to Yazid that says the fighting lasted no longer than a siesta, concluded that there was a quick massacre and that the detailed accounts are Iraqi fabrications. Laura Veccia Vaglieri replied that the contemporary accounts together form "a coherent and credible narrative", and Madelung and Wellhausen hold that the battle lasted from sunrise to sunset. Wellhausen thought Yazid's compassion for the family was only for show and put responsibility on Yazid, while Madelung argues that Yazid diverted blame onto Ibn Ziyad.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Karbala&#8221; cites where it covers Primary and classic sources, Historical analysis, Modern historical views on Husayn's motivations:</p><ul><li>S. M. Jafri, Origins and Early Development of Shi'a Islam (Longman, 1979)</li><li>James Howard-Johnston, Witnesses to a World Crisis: Historians and Histories of the Middle East in the Seventh Century (Oxford University Press, 2010)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Heinz Halm writes that "There was no religious aspect to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiism">Shi'ism</a> prior to 680", and that Husayn's death was the "big bang" that created Shi'ism. The battle gave the pro-Alid political party a distinct religious identity. Shia Muslims commemorate Husayn's death in the ten days of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muharram">Muharram</a>, culminating on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashura">Ashura</a>, with gatherings and processions, and in some places self-flagellation, which many Shia scholars have criticised. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> Husayn Shrine at Karbala is holy in Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and the Arba'in pilgrimage there is reported to draw up to 40 million people. Shia Muslims believe that Husayn's death was a sacrifice to protect Islam from corruption by tyrannical rulers, that he knew his fate, and that it was divinely ordained.</p><p>Historians differ on Husayn's motives. Wellhausen called the revolt premature and ill-prepared, and Halm saw a struggle for leadership between second-generation Muslims. Donner, Hawting and Kennedy see an attempt to regain what Hasan had renounced. Vaglieri and Madelung describe a man moved by religious conviction, and Madelung adds that he did not seek martyrdom and wanted to return when expected support failed. Jafri holds that Husayn aimed from the start at martyrdom to shake the Muslim community.</p><p>The battle was used politically soon after. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> claimed to have avenged Husayn by overthrowing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> promoted its rituals. In the 20th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhollah-khomeini">Ruhollah Khomeini</a> compared the Shah to Yazid, and Karbala was reinterpreted as a model of resistance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athtar">Attar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a> also wrote of Husayn.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Karbala&#8221; cites where it covers Impact in Shia Islam, Shia observances, Politics:</p><ul><li>Kamran S. Aghaie, The Martyrs of Karbala: Shi'i Symbols and Rituals in Modern Iran (University of Washington Press, 2004)</li><li>Mahmoud Ayoub, Redemptive Suffering in Islam: A Study of the Devotional Aspects of Ashura in Twelver Shi'ism (Mouton Publishers, 1978)</li><li>Saïd A. Arjomand, Sociology of Shiʿite Islam: Collected Essays (E. J. Brill, 2016)</li><li>Rainer Brunner, Karbala (Princeton University Press, 2013)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>On 10 October 680 at Karbala, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a>, grandson of Muhammad, was killed with most of his companions by the army of the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazid-i">Yazid I</a>. The accounts of the battle are disputed in detail, and it is central to Shia history and tradition.</em></p>

